  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 846 PM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nSNOW TO CONTINUE THROUGH SATURDAY MORNING:\n\nLow pressure is situated over the Great Basin tonight, and as a\nresult, modest moisture continues to flow into our region amid \nthe southwesterly flow. This has and will continue to support \nscattered showers tonight and through the majority of Saturday \nmorning. The trough axis swings through near mid-day tomorrow, \nquickly shutting off the tap of moisture and ending the majority\nof active weather in our CWA. Until then, look for another one \nto three tenths of liquid precipitation to fall across the San \nJuans, central Colorado mountains, and along US-50 (generally \neast of Montrose). Cooler temperatures will continue to support \nlower snow levels, and so with most remaining precipitation \nexpected over mountains and mountain valleys, snow should \ndominate throughout the end of this event. Additional \naccumulations will generally be under 3 inches, though some \nlocally higher amounts are possible on the northern slopes of \nthe San Juans where snowfall is expected to be more persistent \nthrough the night. Otherwise, temperatures have notably dropped \nbehind the frontal boundary which passed through today, which \nmeans overnight lows for the next several night are tracking to\ndrop below freezing for a large swath of the region. Any valley\nlocations which have yet to hit freezing temperatures should \nexpect to drop below freezing as a result.\n\nLIGHT SHOWERS EARLY NEXT WEEK:\n\nA low-amplitude ridge builds in behind the exiting trough \nSaturday evening into Sunday morning. Skies may clear out some \nduring the overnight period, which would support a localized fog\nrisk in some valleys, though the current forecast isn't overly \nbullish on widespread saturation at the surface. Chances for fog\nare highest further north in the Uinta and Yampa Basins though.\nBy Sunday evening, upper-level flow will be turning back \nsouthwesterly ahead of another approaching trough. Moisture and \nupper-level support is unimpressive with this storm, so current \nexpectations are for only light showers Sunday evening through \nTuesday morning. Snow accumulations are on track to sit well \nbelow advisory level.\n\nThere is a vague signal of another system passing through in \nthe latter half of next week, but cluster analysis shows a wide \nrange of outcomes with low confidence on when wet weather would \narrive, if at all. Though confidence is extremely low at this \ntime, this potential storm could be impactful to Thanksgiving \ntravel. Stay tuned over the coming days for additional updates \nregarding the late-week weather, especially if you are planning \nto travel for the holiday.",
